 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to commercial\nfood fish licenses",
    "basePrintNo": "S10044",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S10044-2025",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §13-0328, En Con L",
    "lawSection": "Environmental Conservation Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S10044\n\nSPONSOR: HARCKHAM\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to\ncommercial food fish licenses\n\n \nPURPOSE OF THE BILL:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to extend the Department's authority to\nissue limited entry commercial fishing licenses and permits.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of this bill amends Environmental Conservation Law (ECL) §\n13-0328 authorizing the Department to: 1) through December 31, 2029,\nmaintain a yearly cap on the number of commercial food fish, crab, whelk\n(conch), and Marine and Coastal District Party and Charter Boat licenses\nor permits that may be issued; 2) through December 31, 2029, maintain\nthe current moratorium on new lobster permits; and 3) through December\n31, 2029, maintain current license eligibility requirements.\n\nSection 2 of the proposed bill provides for an immediate effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis bill would maintain current license and permit limits. A complete\nmoratorium will be maintained on commercial lobster permits. A substan-\ntial decline in lobster abundance has resulted from episodes of mortal-\nity beginning in 1999, supporting a continued moratorium to prevent new\nentrants in this troubled fishery.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nDEC's authority was last extended by Chapter 306 of the Laws of 2023.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
